            # compare\n                # Compare: higher score >; if equal, fewer tokens >; if equal, lexicographically smaller indices >\n                if (take[0], -take[1], take[2]) > (best[0], -best[1], best[2]):\n                    best = take\n            dp[i+1][cap] = best\n    # after all items, find best among dp[n][0..budget]\n    best_overall = (0.0, 0, [])\n    for cap in range(budget+1):\n        candidate = dp[n][cap]\n        if (candidate[0], -candidate[1], candidate[2]) > (best_overall[0], -best_overall[1], best_overall[2]):\n            best_overall = candidate\n    selected_indices = best_overall[2]\n    tokens_used = best_overall[1]\n    total_score = best_overall[0]\n    return {\"selected_indices\": selected_indices, \"tokens_used\": tokens_used, \"total_score\": total_score}\n\nBut note: The output must have total_score as number (float). tokens_used as integer.\n\nCheck: The DP as implemented may have issues with floating point equality? Scores are floats, but equality might be rare. If equal scores, we use negative tokens to compare. The lexicographic comparison of indices lists uses Python default list comparison, which works element-wise. Since indices are increasing, it's fine.\n\nMemory: each dp row contains budget+1 tuples; each tuple has a list which is shared? Actually when we do prev[2] + [i], it creates a new list. That's O(n*budget) lists, each of length up to n. That could be heavy. But we can store only the decision (taken or not) and reconstruct later. Better: store only (score, tokens) and then backtrack to get indices. For tie-breaking, we need to know which path was chosen. We can store for each dp cell the previous cell index and whether taken. But we also need to break ties during DP. We can store (score, tokens) and also a comparison key using indices? But indices are not known until reconstruction. However, the tie-breaking rule uses the actual index list. That means we cannot decide purely based on score and tokens; 